From ccbd7f625c4f5d501a5be0ab6fe282a2c5abc3ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:50:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(kubevirt): build vm_create's VirtualMachine as data,
 not templated YAML

vm_create rendered its arguments into vm.yaml.tmpl with text/template,
without escaping, and passed the text to the multi-document apply,
which splits on `---` and force-applies every document. The workload
argument is passed through unchanged when it looks like an image
reference and ends up on the template's last line, so a workload value
containing a newline, a separator and a manifest made vm_create apply
that extra object too, of any kind and in any namespace the server
identity may write, and still answer "VirtualMachine created
successfully". Operators who expose only vm_create (enabled_tools) or
only the kubevirt toolset do not expect it to write anything but
VirtualMachines; its RBAC metadata declares only VirtualMachines.

The VirtualMachine is now built as an unstructured object (buildVM),
field by field, with the same content the template produced, and
applied through a new Core.ResourcesApplyObject that applies exactly
one object and parses no manifest text. Every argument is a scalar
field value and cannot add fields or documents. vm.yaml.tmpl is
removed. In addition, a workload containing whitespace or control
characters is refused up front, since neither an OS name nor an image
reference can contain them.

TestRenderVMYaml asserted substrings of the rendered template; it is
replaced by TestBuildVM, which asserts the object's fields (including
that manifest text in an argument stays a field value). An MCP test
checks that a workload carrying a second document is refused and
creates nothing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
